// ============================================================
// WRITTEN ANSWERS
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Why do L2 and L3 start from users?
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// If we started from appointments, we would only see users
// who already have at least one appointment. Dr. Sofia
// Lindqvist and Eva Nilsson would disappear from the results.
// Starting from users keeps them in the results, and $lookup
// gives them an empty appointments array.

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Why can't the validator guarantee that doctorId belongs
// to a doctor? How does L7(b) detect the problem?
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The validator can check that doctorId is an ObjectId, but
// it cannot check the role of a document in another collection.
// In L7(b), $lookup finds the referenced user and we check
// whether that user's role is "patient". The application can
// prevent this by checking that the selected user exists and
// has the doctor role before creating an appointment.

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. One advantage and one disadvantage of storing doctors
// and patients in the same users collection.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// One advantage is that common information, such as name,
// email and phone, is stored in one place. One disadvantage
// is that relationships become more complicated because we
// must check the role of a referenced user to know whether
// that user is really a doctor or a patient.

// ============================================================
